{black is up an exchange, if I can stabilise I'm probably better. 

5 min candidate move check: Qxd1, Qxe7, Ng4, Qc7 (mate threats)

&nbsp;

Qc7, Nxe4, ng4, Nf6+, Nxf6 and white wins back the material

&nbsp;

Qxe7, Nxe4, Ng4, Qe3, Qxe4, fxe4 (black threatens the bishop and a fork) -4. 

Qxe7, Nxe4, Ng4, Nf6+, Qxf6, Qd2, Black is up a pawn but probably still a draw

&nbsp;

Ng4, exd8+, rxd8, rxd8+ (this line is nonsense).

&nbsp;

Qxd1, Nxd1, Ng4 (queen is overloaded), Qd2, Nxh6, Qd8+, Kg7, e8 = N, Rexe8

and if after kg7, Qxc8 then re1++

Qxd1, Nxd1, Ng4, Qd2, Nxh3, Nc3, Rxe7 and white still can't take the knight back. 

&nbsp;

verdict: correct firsst move! but wrong second move, the full line I saw fails. due to h3 at the end killing the backrank threats. My mistake was not expanding candidate moves after my first move. I missed a pretty obvious follow up because I assumed ng4 "had to be the right second move" but Nd3 clearly wins on the spot. overall lesson is that it's much more worthwhile spending more time expanding candidate moves down a variation rather than going deep on just one line. } 1... Qxd1+! 2. Nxd1 Nd3! {the move I missed. wins on the spot. }  (2... Ng4  3. Qd2 Nxh6 4. Qd8+ Kg7 5. e8=Q?  (5. h3! {but white does not have to promote straight away. }  )) *

